+++
title: Chogha Zanbil
description: Ancient Elamite complex in Khuzestan Province, Iran
hook: About 1250 BC an Elamite king built a walled town, named it after
  himself — Dur Untash, 'town of Untash' — and dedicated it mainly to the god
  Inshushinak. It is unlikely that many people besides priests and servants
  ever lived there.
standfirst: "An Elamite complex in Khuzestan, Iran, about 30 km southeast of
  Susa: one of the few existing ziggurats outside Mesopotamia, and the one
  UNESCO considers the best preserved example of the stepped pyramidal
  monument. In 1979 it became the first Iranian site on the World Heritage
  List."
key-facts:
  - name: Built
    value: about 1250 BC, by the king Untash-Napirisha
    from: It was built about 1250 BC by the king [[ Untash-Napirisha ]], mainly to
      honor the great god [[ Inshushinak ]].
  - name: Where
    value: the Khuzestan province of Iran, approximately 30 km (19 mi) southeast
      of Susa and 80 km (50 mi) north of Ahvaz
    from: "!!Chogha Zanbil!![[ ^a ]] is an ancient [[ Elamite | elam ]] complex in
      the [[ Khuzestan ]] province of [[ Iran ]]. It is one of the few
      existing [[ ziggurats | ziggurat ]] outside [[ Mesopotamia ]]. It lies
      approximately 30 km (19 mi) southeast of [[ Susa ]] and 80 km (50 mi)
      north of [[ Ahvaz ]]."
  - name: End of the city
    value: in about 645 BC — the city is currently believed to have been destroyed
      then by Assurbanipal, though some researchers place the end of
      occupation in the late 12th century BC
    from: The city is currently believed to have been destroyed by the [[
      Neo-Assyrian ]] ruler [[ Assurbanipal ]] in about 645 BC, along with the
      Elamite capital of [[ Susa ]], though some researchers place the end of
      occupation in the late 12th century BC.[[ ^1 ]]
  - name: The ziggurat
    value: originally 105.2 metres (345 ft) on each side and about 53 metres (174
      ft) high in five levels; it now stands 24.75 metres (81.2 ft) high
    from: The ziggurat originally measured 105.2 metres (345 ft) on each side and
      about 53 metres (174 ft) in height, in five levels, and was crowned with
      a temple. Mud brick was the basic material of the whole ensemble. The
      ziggurat was given a facing of baked bricks, a number of which have
      cuneiform characters giving the names of deities in the Elamite and
      Akkadian languages. Though the ziggurat now stands only 24.75 metres
      (81.2 ft) high, less than half its estimated original height, its state
      of preservation is unsurpassed.
  - name: World Heritage Site
    value: "inscribed 1979 (3rd Session) as Tchogha Zanbil, reference 113, under
      criteria Cultural: (iii), (iv)"
    from: |-
      unesco-world-heritage-site:
          official-name: Tchogha Zanbil
          criteria: "Cultural: (iii), (iv)"
          reference: "113"
          inscription: 1979 (3rd Session)
