      Neo-Assyrian ]] ruler [[ Assurbanipal ]] in about 645 BC, along with the
      Elamite capital of [[ Susa ]], though some researchers place the end of
      occupation in the late 12th century BC.[[ ^1 ]]
  - when: 1935–39 and 1946
    what: Roland de Mecquenem surveys and excavates the site.
    from: It was surveyed and excavated by Roland de Mecquenem in 1935–39 and
      1946.[[ ^7 ]]
  - when: 1951 and 1962
    what: Roman Ghirshman excavates in six seasons, 21 months in all, for the
      French Archeological Mission.
    from: Chogha Zanbil was excavated in six seasons (a total of 21 months)
      between 1951 and 1962 by [[ Roman Ghirshman ]] with the French
      Archeological Mission.
  - when: 1970-01-26
    what: Designated Iranian National Heritage.
    from: |-
      - value: Iranian National Heritage
              from: 1970-01-26
  - when: "1979"
    what: Chogha Zanbil becomes the first Iranian site inscribed on the UNESCO
      World Heritage List.
    from: In 1979, Chogha Zanbil became the first Iranian site to be inscribed on
      the [[ UNESCO ]] [[ World Heritage List ]].
  - when: 1999, 2002, 2004, and 2005
    what: Behzad Mofidi Nasrabadi excavates, including a 35 ha (86 acres)
      magnetometer survey by the University of Kiel.
    from: Most recently the site was excavated by Behzad Mofidi Nasrabadi in 1999,
      2002, 2004, and 2005. This included a 35 ha (86 acres) magnetometer
      survey by the [[ University of Kiel ]][[ ^17 ]][[ ^18 ]][[ ^19 ]]
