glossary:
  - term: ziggurat
    gloss: A stepped pyramidal monument; UNESCO considers the one here the best
      preserved example of the form.
    from: The ziggurat is considered to be the best preserved example of the
      stepped pyramidal monument by [[ UNESCO ]].[[ ^2 ]]
  - term: Chogha Zanbil
    gloss: The modern name of the site, typically translated as 'basket mound.'
    from: //Chogha Zanbil// is typically translated as 'basket mound.'
  - term: Dur Untash
    gloss: The city's original name, meaning 'town of Untash'.
    from: Its original name was //Dur Untash//, which means 'town of Untash', but
      it is unlikely that many people, besides priests and servants, ever
      lived there.
  - term: Untash-Napirisha
    gloss: The king who built the city; an inscribed brick from the site calls him
      son of Humbanumena, king of Anšan and of Susa.
    from: '"I, Untaš-Napiriša, son of Humbanumena, king of Anšan and of Susa,
      anxious for my life to be continually prosperous, so that I may noy be
      granted the extinction of my prosperous lineage, a temple of baked
      bricks and a sanctuary of glazed bricks I built; I gave it to Inšušinak
      of the Siyan-kuk and I a temple tower erected. May what I made and
      toiled for, as a gift on my behalf, be acceptable to Inšušinak!"[[ ^16
      ]]'
  - term: Inshushinak
    gloss: The great god the city was mainly built to honor, and to whom the great
      ziggurat was dedicated.
    from: It was built about 1250 BC by the king [[ Untash-Napirisha ]], mainly to
      honor the great god [[ Inshushinak ]].
  - term: Napirisha
    gloss: The chief Elamite god, who shared a temple between the outer and middle
      walls with Inshushinak.
    from: The middle wall had four gates and between the outer and middle walls
      was a shared temple dedicated to the chief Elamite god [[ Napirisha ]]
      and to Inshushinak.[[ ^3 ]]
  - term: Elamite
    gloss: "One of the two languages of the site's brick inscriptions: of the
      5,257 inscribed bricks found, most are in Elamite and 67 in Akkadian."
    from: A number of inscribed bricks were found (5,257 in total with most in
      Elamite but with 67 in Akkadian).
  - term: Untash-Gal
    gloss: The name in the first inscription taken from the site, described as the
      builder of a sacred city.
    from: The inscription described the building of a sacred city by one
      "Untash-Gal".[[ ^1 ]]
further:
